but in this original code it ignores the Intent and uses the hardcoded username "test" which breaks the multi-user support

56
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:25,633
So I will follow four steps to enhance this artifact.

57
00:07:27,333 --> 00:07:36,633
Step one fix the data layer by updating DAO queries. Step two, create a single source of truth with a WeightRepository class. 

58
00:07:37,533 --> 00:07:42,733
For step three I will fix the ViewModel layer to hold the UI state. And fourth

59
00:07:43,266 --> 00:07:52,200
I will update the UI later by refactoring the DashboardActivity instead of the database directly.

60
00:07:53,833 --> 00:08:04,733
The new flow will be -> user logs in, MainActivity sends credentials to the ViewModel, ViewModel asks repository for history per username, repository asks DAO where username = ? using an index

61
00:08:04,733 --> 00:08:08,266
DAO returns rows, repository cleans 

62
00:08:09,033 --> 00:08:19,066
nulls and converts to TreeMap, ViewModel posts to LiveData. Which will help show modular design and defensive programming.

63
00:08:19,866 --> 00:08:25,333
For category two which is Algorithms and Data Structures

64
00:08:27,933 --> 00:08:32,266
when the user opens the history screen, the code calls History 

65
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:43,233
which does DatabaseHelper SELECT FROM weights ORDER BY id. But returns every row for the user "test"

66
00:08:44,833 --> 00:08:48,000
Then in here, HistoryActivity

67
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:57,633
So I load Big O of n data (which is everything) to display just five. Which means I query and load all rows just to show five

68
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:02,066
which wastes memory and slows down the application

69
00:09:03,066 --> 00:09:09,866
So for my second enhancement I will be refactoring to a balanced BST and adding a range query algorithm.

70
00:09:10,733 --> 00:09:16,833
So as I said the bottleneck is a full scan

71
00:09:17,333 --> 00:09:22,600
plus expensive date parsing inside loop.
To mitigate this problem I have four steps again,

72
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:30,766
I will refactor storage to use BST - specifically TreeMap sorted by date to give me Big O log n 

73
00:09:31,066 --> 00:09:38,466
Step two, I will add range query for efficiency, so I only get dates that I need.

74
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:44,933
Step three - Update the weight calculations and modify the notifications

75
00:09:47,266 --> 00:09:51,166
Step four, update UI to display new records more efficiently.

76
00:09:51,166 --> 00:09:56,500
So the new flow -> user opens the history, then the weights are stored in TreeMap 

77
00:09:57,133 --> 00:10:02,266
Sorted by date. And show records from last five days are gathered

78
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,066
Then I calculate variance and notify if the variance is less than 1 lb.

79
00:10:07,066 --> 00:10:09,100
Meaning there's a stable weight.

80
00:10:10,733 --> 00:10:17,766
Before it would be a Big O of n, and after would be Big O of log n for search.

81
00:10:17,766 --> 00:10:20,333
 Plus Big O of k for display.

82
00:10:21,133 --> 00:10:25,133
Then for my category three - The databases

83
00:10:26,666 --> 00:10:30,833
DatabaseHelper manages fitness.db with users and weights 

84
00:10:32,933 --> 00:10:35,333
It has registerUser,

85
00:10:35,666 --> 00:10:40,833
it has loginUser, addWeight, getLastTarget, getHistory and deleteWeight.

86
00:10:41,900 --> 00:10:48,766
When adding weight, it inserts without a foreign key

87
00:10:50,700 --> 00:10:55,333
so when Dashboard calls weights, the database scans every single row to find test because there is no index.

88
00:10:56,300 --> 00:11:04,400
the goals column inside weights table has no relation to users and allows duplicates because username is not a primary key.

89
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:10,800
For my third enhancement I will normalize and design with Room and enforce data integrity.

90
00:11:12,033 --> 00:11:20,566
As I said the current problem is that I have a single SQLite helper, with no foreign keys, no indexes, and plain text password stored.

91
00:11:21,800 --> 00:11:26,000
I will follow these four steps to enhance this artifact. 

92
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:34,266
Step one will be refactoring to room. Step two will be creating
a user's table with username as the primary key

93
00:11:34,766 --> 00:11:40,133
Step three will be implementing password hashing with BCrypt for security,

94
00:11:40,133 --> 00:11:40,900
and step four -

95
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:50,900
create weights table using foreign key with cascade delete, and create indexes on username and date for the Big O log n performance.

96
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:59,400
The new flow will be -> user registers, password is hashed with BCrypt and saved in users table where the username is a primary key,

97
00:12:00,700 --> 00:12:03,066
inserting duplicate will fail due to constraint.

98
00:12:03,833 --> 00:12:11,700
Insert weight into weights table with foreign key, so insert fails if the username does not exist.

99
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:15,566
Before, anyone could see same data and full scan Big O of n. 

100
00:12:16,366 --> 00:12:27,800
Then after, secure indexed Big O of log n, and data integrity enforced. This strenghtes security and database design.

